A stop that timed out must not report success.

vl53l7cx_stop_ranging() provokes an MCU stop and then polls G02 status 0 for bit 7,
for up to five seconds. On timeout it does `status |= tmp` and breaks -- where `tmp`
is the last byte read, and the loop can only still be running because bit 7 was
clear. So `tmp` is in 0x00..0x7F, and a device that keeps answering 0x00 -- the
ordinary reading for "not stopped", and the reading a bus that is simply not
answering also produces through a zeroing port -- leaves `status` exactly as it was.
Five seconds of waiting then returns VL53L7CX_STATUS_OK.

The check immediately below does not save it either: it re-reads 0x6 and only looks
at register 0x7 when bit 7 IS set, so a device that never stopped is passed over in
silence.

This matters here more than it does upstream. The boot-time recovery pass exists to
put a surviving sensor into a known state over I2C, and it decides what to report
from this return value. "Stopped" when nothing was confirmed is the one answer it
must not produce: it turns an unrecovered survivor into a clean boot record, and the
next failure is attributed somewhere else.

The timeout is therefore made a failure in its own right, with the status the ULD
already defines for it. `status |= tmp` is kept rather than replaced: on the paths
where tmp carries a device error code that code is still worth propagating.

Applied to the copy in the build directory, never in place, so SHA256SUMS continues
to answer whether upstream changed.
diff --git a/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c b/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c
index 9d3d3eb..271b2db 100644
--- a/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c
+++ b/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c
@@ -646,8 +646,9 @@ uint8_t vl53l7cx_stop_ranging(
 
 	        	if(timeout > (uint16_t)500)
 				{
 					status |= tmp;
+					status |= VL53L7CX_STATUS_TIMEOUT_ERROR;
 					break;
 				}
         	}
         }
